HST Office: leave a note when the operating system ends the office, so the next start does not take
a shut down, restart or log off for a crash.

Measured on 1.0.1 and rc/1.0.2-batch5 (Linux Xvfb, Windows 11 HST4): a session the system ended looked
exactly like a kill at the next start: the session marker of the bundled extension was still there, so it
asked "Lần trước HST Office bị đóng đột ngột. Gửi thông tin…" on top of the recovery dialog.
- Linux: systemd stops the user session with SIGTERM (log out, shut down; KDE and other desktops without
  the GNOME session protocol). sal's handler ends the process with _exit(255) at once. It now writes
  "Terminated: Signal 15" to stderr first (write(2), async-signal-safe); the extension keeps the stderr of
  a desktop start in last-session.err.
- Windows: WM_QUERYENDSESSION runs the normal quit with its save questions; when a question is still open
  (or for a forced restart) Windows ends the process, and after WM_ENDSESSION(TRUE) it ends it in any
  case. The first WM_QUERYENDSESSION now appends "session-end=query" to the note file named by
  HST_FAULT_NOTE (hst-windows-silent-crash-note.patch), WM_ENDSESSION "session-end=end" or, when the log
  off was cancelled, "session-end=cancel".
The extension (session_end.py) reads the note at the next start: no crash report question for such a
session; document recovery still follows the office's own recovery state.
